            <ArticleTitle>[A CASE OF ASPIRIN-EXACERBATED RESPIRATORY DISEASE COMPLICATED BY REFRACTORY VARIANT ANGINA, WHICH SHOWED EXCELLENT RESPONSE TO STEROID TREATMENT].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>123-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.15036/arerugi.65.123</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 47-year-old woman with aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease visited our hospital complaining of persistent chest pain that manifested in the evenings and early mornings. Holter monitoring revealed ST elevation during chest pain and coronary angiography showed coronary vasospasm, which led to the diagnosis of variant angina. Chest pain persisted despite administration of a coronary vasodilator. The patient experienced an increase in peripheral blood eosinophils during the clinical course and received prednisolone for the same, which resulted in the resolution of her chest pain. Prednisolone was therefore seen to be effective for treating variant angina that manifested as a non-respiratory tract symptom of aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
